# Intent: pets that register, from the host log of the petting minutes (2026-10-21). With nobody there the strip reads 1 on 60 to 90 samples a minute, in runs up to 23 samples long, so reading 1 is noise however long it lasts; real pats and rubs reach 2 or 3. 052's "dense contact at reading 1" would have turned that noise into pets, and its blip lockout locked out real pets (blips=10 with presses=0). A pet is now a run of touched samples whose peak reading is at least 2 and that lasts at least 2 samples (a pat of 3 samples at peak 3 counts; a single sample or any run that never passes 1 does not). Every such run is one press, so a stroke is several of them; a run ends after 2 untouched samples in a row, so one dropout inside a rub does not split it. There is no lockout: weak runs never count. head_touch_stats reports weak and strong samples and runs apart (weak, strong, runs_weak, runs_strong, run_max, strong_run_max), so the noise floor and a pat can be read off the host log. Press and swipe events carry strong=1 so the host trusts each. The strip's sensitivity is two constants at the top of the file (kSensitivityType, kSensitivityLevel), unchanged. The boot report now carries crash_resets, a count of panic and watchdog restarts kept in NVS, so a stack or memory change that makes the unit reset shows as a rising number.
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal_head_touch.cpp, hal/hal_mcp.cpp (MIT). Applies after 055 with --unidiff-zero.
# Check after a flash: idle 5 minutes: presses=0, runs_weak may be non-zero, no pets. Pat the head hard and soft, short and long: presses rises by one per pat or stroke contact and the host reacts to each. Boot: "unit boot: boot reason=1 crash_resets=0".
